        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="true" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="0" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>At the time of the inspection we notice some air leakage on the following house components:

-Window in basement

-Garage, living room and dining room doors

-Electrical receptacles

-Furnace chimney

-Crawl space

We recommend the replacement or addition of weather strips to seal the doors. The same solution is applied to seal the electrical receptacles. Unfortunately, there is nothing to do to reduce the air leakage from the furnace chimney, unless you are planning the replacement of the heating system by a direct and sealed unit.

Concerning the crawl space, the addition of insulation will help to increase heat loss from this section. 

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>The actual insulation level of your attic is R-28,  we recommend the addition of insulation to meet the city of Whitehorse standard of R-60. 

The addition of insulation will increase your capacity to retain heat into the living space and reduce heat loss during winter. In the summertime, the addition of insulation will slow down the migration of heat from the attic to the upper floor. 

</ceilings>
                <foundation>We recommend to increase the insulation level of your foundations' wall. You are eligible for $2.50/square inch for the addition of R-20+, if the addition is of R-30+ the rebate is $ 3.50/square inch. </foundation>
                <windows>We recommend the replacement of the wood window in the basement by an Energy Star product suited for the northern climate. </windows>
                <VentilationSystem>The result of the infiltration test has shown that your house's natural air exchange is not enough. We recommend the installation of an HRV (Heat Recovery Ventilator) HVI (Home Ventilation Institute) and Energy Star certified, to ensure proper ventilation inside your home.</VentilationSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
